 ## Building
 
 ### The engine
 
+Everything below is run from the app checkout. It needs a Rust toolchain; the
+version that matters is whatever `rustc --version` reports, and nothing here
+pins one.
+
 ```bash
-# From a checkout next to pixelsmith:
-bash native/build-engine.sh --release
+# Desktop, the host platform (Linux, macOS or Windows):
+bash native/build-engine.sh
+bash native/build-engine.sh --release            # optimised
+
+# Android. Opt-in: the target is never inferred from the environment.
+bash native/build-engine.sh --target android --release
 
-# Or against an explicit engine checkout:
-bash native/build-engine.sh --release --engine-dir /path/to/pixelsmith
+# Against an explicit engine checkout rather than a sibling directory:
+bash native/build-engine.sh --engine-dir /path/to/pixelsmith
+```
+
+Outputs, and where each platform's loader looks for them:
+
+| Target | Command | Artefact |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Linux | `bash native/build-engine.sh --release` | `src/rust/libpixelsmith_core.so` |
+| macOS | `bash native/build-engine.sh --release` | `src/rust/libpixelsmith_core.dylib` |
+| Windows | `bash native/build-engine.sh --release` | `src/rust/pixelsmith_core.dll` |
+| Android | `bash native/build-engine.sh --target android --release` | `src/rust/jniLibs/<abi>/libpixelsmith_core.so` |
+| iOS | `cargo lipo -o src/rust/libpixelsmith_core.a` then link it in the Xcode target | static |
+
+**Android** needs `cargo-ndk` (the script installs it if it is missing) and
+`ANDROID_NDK_HOME`. Plain `cargo build --target aarch64-linux-android` fails at
+link time with no linker, which is the reason cargo-ndk exists here.
+
+**iOS** is the one target this script does not build: `cargo-lipo` merges the
+device and simulator slices into a static library, and the Xcode target links it.
+
+```bash
+rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios aarch64-apple-ios-sim x86_64-apple-ios
+cargo install cargo-lipo
+cargo lipo -t aarch64-apple-ios -t aarch64-apple-ios-sim \
+  -t x86_64-apple-ios --release -o src/rust/libpixelsmith_core.a
 ```
 
-Requires a Rust toolchain. Android cross-compiles need `cargo-ndk` (the script
-installs it); plain `cargo build --target aarch64-linux-android` fails at link
-time with no linker, which is what the script exists to avoid.
+None of these artefacts are committed. They are in `.gitignore` and CI rebuilds
+them on every run.
+
+**Desktop builds compile the engine themselves.** `linux/CMakeLists.txt` and
+`windows/CMakeLists.txt` run `native/build-engine.sh` as part of every build and
+install the result into the bundle, so `flutter build linux` and
+`flutter build windows` need no manual step:
+
+```bash
+flutter build linux          # the engine is built and copied into bundle/lib
+flutter build windows --release   # into the directory holding the EXE
+```
+
+A Debug build gets a debug engine and everything else gets `--release`. Point the
+build at a specific engine checkout with
+`-DPIXELSMITH_ENGINE_DIR=/path/to/pixelsmith`; left empty, `build-engine.sh`
+infers it from the layout (a sibling `../pixelsmith`, or `../core` when this
+repository is the `app/` submodule). If `bash` is not on `PATH` the configure step
+fails outright rather than producing a bundle with no engine in it.
+
+**macOS is not wired up the same way, and the gap is deliberate.** The Apple
+targets link the engine statically (`DynamicLibrary.process()`), and the Xcode
+project has no build phase that runs `build-engine.sh`, so a macOS bundle needs
+`bash native/build-engine.sh --release` plus a link step in the Xcode target.
+That is a decision for whoever can run Xcode, not a line to guess at here.
+
+### Finding the library at runtime
+
+`loadEngineLibrary` searches, in order: `src/rust/`, then the directory holding
+the executable and `lib/` beside it, then the platform loader path, and on Apple
+platforms `DynamicLibrary.process()` because the engine is linked in
+statically. The order matters because `DynamicLibrary.open('name.so')` searches
+the *loader path*, not the working directory: a library sitting in the project is
+invisible to it, which is why a desktop `flutter run` used to fail with a dlopen
+error while the APK worked fine.
+
+`flutter run` from a checkout does not copy the library anywhere, so on Linux it
+still needs the loader path pointed at it:
+
+```bash
+LD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/src/rust" flutter run -d linux
+```
 
